(b) Bundling, embedding or incorporating the Software into another product,
    application, plugin, device or distribution; rebranding it; or offering
    a dedicated wrapper that presents its processing as another product.
(c) Making the Software's processing capabilities available to third parties
    through a hosted service, website, API or other processing platform.

These requirements apply whether the product or service is paid or free,
whether the Software is included or downloaded separately, and whether it
runs locally or on a server. A separate process or MCP/CLI connection does
not by itself exempt a product or service from this section.

Using a general-purpose MCP client or private internal automation for your
own production work remains permitted under section 2. Delivering Outputs
to clients as part of your creative production work is permitted under
section 3, including when you automate that work internally. Offering
third parties access to processing functions as a product or service is
subject to this section. Merely publishing ordinary connection settings
for a general-purpose client does not constitute a dedicated wrapper.

No integration, redistribution or hosted-service license is granted by
requesting a quote or offering payment. Its scope, price and other terms
